\title{Galois Connections Between Order Theory and Topology}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 2, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a direct bridge between order theory and topology through Galois connections. Every preorder carries an upper Alexandrov topology whose open sets are the upward-closed subsets. A map between preorders is continuous for these topologies if and only if it is monotone. Since both adjoints in a Galois connection are monotone, every Galois connection yields topologies on its two underlying preorders for which both adjoints are continuous. When the domain is a complete lattice, the composite of the adjoints is an extensive, monotone, idempotent closure operator, and its fixed points form a complete lattice; meets are inherited from the ambient lattice, while joins are obtained by closing ambient joins. We specialize this construction to the ideal--zero-locus correspondence for the prime spectrum of a commutative ring. The Galois law characterizes Zariski closed sets, and the ideal-side closure is radicalization. Finally, an explicit closure on a three-point set fails preservation of binary unions, showing that a general order-theoretic closure need not be topological.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Order and topology encode different forms of structure. An order records refinement, implication, containment, or increasing information. A topology records observability, local behavior, and continuity. These languages interact cleanly on preordered sets: upward persistence can be interpreted as openness, and under this interpretation monotonicity becomes continuity.

A Galois connection between preorders $P$ and $Q$ consists of maps $l:P\to Q$ and $u:Q\to P$ such that
\[
l(x)\le y \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\le u(y).
\]
The first objective of this paper is to make the topological content of this pattern explicit. Every preorder admits an upper Alexandrov topology, and continuity between such spaces is equivalent to monotonicity. Both adjoints are consequently continuous.

The second objective is to describe stable objects produced by a round trip. The composite $c=u\circ l$ is a closure operator. If $P$ is complete, its fixed points form a complete lattice. The third objective is to instantiate this mechanism in algebraic geometry: ideals and subsets of the prime spectrum are related by zero-locus and vanishing-ideal operations, with radicalization as the induced closure.

A final objective is to mark the boundary of the analogy. An order closure on a powerset need not preserve finite unions and therefore need not arise from a topology. The universally available Alexandrov topology and the potentially non-topological Galois closure are distinct constructions.

\section{The upper Alexandrov topology}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{preorder} is a set $P$ with a reflexive, transitive relation $\leq$. A map $f:P\to Q$ is \emph{monotone} when $x\leq y$ implies $f(x)\leq f(y)$. A subset $U\subseteq P$ is \emph{upward closed} when $x\in U$ and $x\leq y$ imply $y\in U$.
\end{definition}

For $x\in P$, write
\[
\mathord\uparrow x=\{y\in P:x\leq y\}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Upper Alexandrov topology]
For a preorder $P$, define
\[
\tau_\uparrow(P)=\{U\subseteq P:U\text{ is upward closed}\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
The family $\tau_\uparrow(P)$ is a topology on $P$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The whole set is upward closed. If membership in $U$ and $V$ persists upward, then membership in $U\cap V$ does as well. If $x$ belongs to a union of upward-closed sets, it belongs to one member $U_i$; every $y\geq x$ also belongs to that $U_i$, hence to the union. Thus finite intersections and arbitrary unions satisfy the topology axioms.
\end{proof}

The principal upper set $\mathord\uparrow x$ is the smallest open neighborhood of $x$. In fact, arbitrary intersections of upper open sets remain open.

\begin{theorem}[Order--Topology Bridge]
Let $P$ and $Q$ carry their upper Alexandrov topologies. A function $f:P\to Q$ is continuous if and only if it is monotone.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $f$ is monotone and $V\subseteq Q$ is upward closed. If $x\in f^{-1}(V)$ and $x\leq y$, then $f(x)\leq f(y)$, so upward closure of $V$ gives $f(y)\in V$. Hence $f^{-1}(V)$ is open.

Conversely, suppose $f$ is continuous and $x\leq y$. The set $\mathord\uparrow f(x)$ is open. Its inverse image is open, contains $x$, and is therefore upward closed; it must contain $y$. Thus $f(y)\in\mathord\uparrow f(x)$, meaning $f(x)\leq f(y)$.
\end{proof}

On finite preorders, this theorem replaces a potentially exponential enumeration of upper sets with a scan of comparable pairs.

\section{Galois connections and continuity}

\begin{definition}[Galois connection]
Maps $l:P\to Q$ and $u:Q\to P$ form a Galois connection, written $l\dashv u$, if
\[
l(x)\leq y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\leq u(y)
\]
for every $x\in P$ and $y\in Q$.
\end{definition}

Taking $y=l(x)$ and $x=u(y)$ gives the unit and counit inequalities
\[
x\leq u(l(x)),\qquad l(u(y))\leq y.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity of adjoints]
Both maps in a Galois connection are monotone.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $x\leq x'$, then $x'\leq u(l(x'))$ and hence $x\leq u(l(x'))$. Adjunction gives $l(x)\leq l(x')$. Dually, if $y\leq y'$, then $l(u(y))\leq y\leq y'$, and adjunction gives $u(y)\leq u(y')$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Continuity of Galois adjoints]
Equip the preorders on both sides of a Galois connection with their upper Alexandrov topologies. Then both the left adjoint and the right adjoint are continuous.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both maps are monotone by the preceding lemma, and monotonicity is equivalent to continuity by the Order--Topology Bridge Theorem.
\end{proof}

The topology is canonical relative to the order. The statement does not claim that it is the unique topology, or the finest or coarsest pair of topologies, making a particular adjoint pair continuous.

\section{Closure and complete lattices of fixed points}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{closure operator} on a preorder $P$ is a map $c:P\to P$ that is extensive, monotone, and idempotent:
\[
x\leq c(x),\qquad x\leq y\Rightarrow c(x)\leq c(y),\qquad c(c(x))=c(x).
\]
An element is \emph{closed} when $c(x)=x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
If $l\dashv u$, then $c=u\circ l$ is a closure operator.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Extensivity is the unit inequality and monotonicity follows from monotonicity of both adjoints. Extensivity applied to $c(x)$ gives $c(x)\leq c(c(x))$. The counit gives $l(u(l(x)))\leq l(x)$; applying $u$ gives the reverse inequality. Hence $c(c(x))=c(x)$.
\end{proof}

Write
\[
\Fix(c)=\{x\in P:c(x)=x\}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-point complete lattice theorem]
Let $P$ be a complete lattice, let $Q$ be a preorder, and let $l:P\to Q$ and $u:Q\to P$ form a Galois connection. Then $\Fix(u\circ l)$ is a complete lattice under the inherited order. For fixed points $x_i$ and $c=u\circ l$,
\[
\bigwedge_{\Fix(c)}x_i=\bigwedge_Px_i,
\qquad
\bigvee_{\Fix(c)}x_i=c\left(\bigvee_Px_i\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $m=\bigwedge_Px_i$. Since $m\leq x_i$, monotonicity gives $c(m)\leq c(x_i)=x_i$ for every $i$, whence $c(m)\leq m$. Extensivity yields equality. Thus the ambient meet is fixed and has the required universal property.

Let $s=\bigvee_Px_i$. Then $c(s)$ is fixed and lies above every $x_i$. If $z$ is a fixed upper bound, then $s\leq z$, so $c(s)\leq c(z)=z$. Therefore $c(s)$ is the least fixed upper bound. The same formulas cover empty families, giving bottom $c(\bot)$ and top $\top$.
\end{proof}

This is the closure-system form of the Knaster--Tarski phenomenon. Raw joins may leave the stable world, but one closure step returns the least stable upper bound.

\begin{algorithm}[Finite Galois closure]
For finite $P$ and $Q$, first check the adjunction on all $|P||Q|$ pairs. Compute $c(x)=u(l(x))$ for every $x\in P$ and retain the elements satisfying $c(x)=x$. Meets are ambient meets; joins are obtained by applying $c$ to ambient joins. With constant-time comparison and evaluation, adjunction testing costs $O(|P||Q|)$ and fixed-point enumeration costs $O(|P|)$, apart from lattice-operation costs.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Prime spectra and Zariski topology}

Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $\Spec(R)$ its set of prime ideals. For an ideal $I\subseteq R$, define
\[
V(I)=\{\mathfrak p\in\Spec(R):I\subseteq\mathfrak p\}.
\]
For $Z\subseteq\Spec(R)$, define
\[
I(Z)=\bigcap_{\mathfrak p\in Z}\mathfrak p.
\]
The maps reverse inclusion: adding equations shrinks the zero locus, while adding points shrinks the ideal common to all points.

\begin{theorem}[Ideal--zero-locus Galois law]
For every ideal $I$ and every subset $Z\subseteq\Spec(R)$,
\[
I\subseteq I(Z)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad Z\subseteq V(I).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first assertion says that each element of $I$ belongs to every prime $\mathfrak p\in Z$. Equivalently, $I\subseteq\mathfrak p$ for every $\mathfrak p\in Z$, which says exactly that every such prime belongs to $V(I)$.
\end{proof}

This is a Galois connection after reversing one order.

\begin{definition}[Zariski topology]
The Zariski topology on $\Spec(R)$ is the topology whose closed sets are the sets
\[
V(S)=\{\mathfrak p\in\Spec(R):S\subseteq\mathfrak p\},
\]
where $S$ ranges over subsets of $R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Characterization of Zariski closed sets]
A subset $Z\subseteq\Spec(R)$ is Zariski closed if and only if $Z=V(I)$ for some ideal $I\subseteq R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By definition, $Z=V(S)$ for some subset $S\subseteq R$. A prime contains $S$ exactly when it contains the ideal $(S)$ generated by $S$, so $V(S)=V((S))$. Conversely, every ideal is a subset of $R$, and therefore every $V(I)$ is closed by definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The radical of an ideal $I$ is
\[
\sqrt I=\{r\in R:\text{there exists }n\geq1\text{ with }r^n\in I\}.
\]
The ideal is radical when $\sqrt I=I$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Radical closure theorem]
For every ideal $I$ in a commutative ring,
\[
I(V(I))=\sqrt I.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The left side is the intersection of all prime ideals containing $I$. If $r^n\in I\subseteq\mathfrak p$ and $\mathfrak p$ is prime, then $r\in\mathfrak p$; hence $\sqrt I$ lies in this intersection. Conversely, if $r\notin\sqrt I$, prime-ideal separation, equivalently localization away from $1,r,r^2,\ldots$, produces a prime ideal containing $I$ but not $r$. Thus $r$ is not in the intersection.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The ideal-side fixed points are precisely the radical ideals. They form a complete lattice: arbitrary meets are intersections, and the join of radical ideals $I_j$ is
\[
\sqrt{\sum_j I_j}.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
For a field $k$, the ideals $(x)$ and $(x^2)$ in $k[x]$ have the same prime zero locus, because a prime contains $x^2$ if and only if it contains $x$. Accordingly,
\[
\sqrt{(x^2)}=(x).
\]
The geometric passage forgets multiplicity, and radicalization records exactly this loss.
\end{example}

\section{A three-point obstruction}

A topological closure on a powerset is extensive, monotone, and idempotent, but also satisfies
\[
\cl(\varnothing)=\varnothing,
\qquad
\cl(A\cup B)=\cl(A)\cup\cl(B).
\]
The latter law need not hold for an order closure.

Let $X=\{0,1,2\}$ and define $c:\mathcal P(X)\to\mathcal P(X)$ by
\[
c(S)=
\begin{cases}
X,&\text{if }0\in S\text{ and }1\in S,\\
S,&\text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]

\begin{proposition}
The map $c$ is an extensive, monotone, idempotent closure operator.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
It is extensive because its value is either $S$ or $X$. If $S\subseteq T$ and $S$ contains both distinguished points, then $T$ does too and both closures are $X$. Otherwise $c(S)=S\subseteq T\subseteq c(T)$, proving monotonicity. Applying the rule twice changes nothing: an expanded set remains $X$, and an unexpanded set still fails the trigger condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of the finite-union law]
The closure $c$ is not the closure operation of any topology on $X$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $A=\{0\}$ and $B=\{1\}$. Then
\[
c(A)\cup c(B)=\{0,1\},
\qquad
c(A\cup B)=X.
\]
The left side omits $2$, so binary-union preservation fails.
\end{proof}

Every subset except $\{0,1\}$ is fixed. The fixed subsets are closed under arbitrary intersections, but not finite unions: $\{0\}$ and $\{1\}$ are fixed while their union is not. Thus a complete lattice of closure-fixed elements need not be the lattice of closed subsets of a topology.

\section{Applications and computation}

For a finite preorder represented by a comparison matrix, upper-Alexandrov continuity of $f$ can be checked by testing $f(x)\leq f(y)$ for every comparable pair. A dense scan takes $O(|P|^2)$ comparisons, whereas enumeration of all opens may be exponential.

For a finite powerset closure, fixed points are enumerated by evaluating $c(S)$ on all subsets. Their meet is intersection and their join is $c$ applied to union. This pattern supports deductive closure, closed pattern mining, and concept-lattice computation.

Equation--solution correspondences have the same architecture. More constraints mean fewer solutions; more solutions mean fewer constraints valid everywhere. Reversing one order produces a Galois connection. A round trip computes semantic closure. On a prime spectrum, semantic closure of an ideal is radicalization, and the fixed descriptions are precisely the radical ideals.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The upper Alexandrov topology answers a universal question: how can an order be regarded as a space so that order-preserving maps become continuous? Open sets express properties stable under upward movement, and continuity is exactly monotonicity. A Galois connection then contributes two structures: continuous adjoint maps and a closure produced by a round trip.

The Zariski case shows that this mechanism carries geometric content. Ideal containment and prime zero loci satisfy the adjunction; zero loci are exactly the closed sets; radicalization is the algebraic round-trip closure. Yet the three-point example prevents an overstatement: order closure is broader than topological closure.

Several problems remain. One may classify the finest and coarsest topology pairs making a fixed adjunction continuous. One may characterize Galois closures on powersets that satisfy the Kuratowski laws; preservation of bottom and binary joins is the expected criterion. The fixed points of $u\circ l$ and $l\circ u$ should be related by an explicit order isomorphism transporting arbitrary meets and joins.

For rings, the correspondence can be upgraded to an order anti-isomorphism between radical ideals and Zariski-closed subsets of $\Spec(R)$, with formulas for all lattice operations. Ring homomorphisms invite a functorial comparison among ideal extension and contraction, maps of prime spectra, and the associated closure operators. Finally, comparing the Alexandrov topology of the specialization order with the Zariski topology would measure how much topology is retained by order alone; they agree for finite spectra but differ in general.

\section{Conclusion}

Every preorder determines an upper Alexandrov topology, and continuity in this topology is exactly monotonicity. Both maps in every Galois connection are therefore continuous. When one side is a complete lattice, fixed points of the adjoint composite form a complete lattice, with inherited meets and closure-corrected joins.

For a commutative ring, zero loci and vanishing ideals realize the framework on the prime spectrum. Their Galois law produces Zariski closed sets, and their round trip on ideals is radicalization. The precise caveat is that an order closure need not preserve finite unions and so need not be topological. This boundary identifies the additional structure required to pass from stable order-theoretic objects to closed subsets of a genuine topology.

